  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 PM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nSurface high pressure centered over western Missouri has allowed for \na drier lower troposphere today. Showers and a few thunderstorms \nmoving east out of central portions of Nebraska and Kansas have  \nstruggled to maintain themselves and this should be the rule through\nlate afternoon, with very little if any rain accumulation. An \nadvancing shortwave over south-central Canada tonight produces modest\nlow-level warm air advection in eastern Kansas late tonight to \nsupport more precipitation. MUCAPE will be greater than today, though\nstill likely less than 1000 J/kg with high storm bases and modest \nflow to limit effective shear despite northwest flow aloft bringing \nsome veering with height. Gusty winds, most likely sub-severe, \nshould be the main convective concern. Some isolated heavier rainfall\ncan't be ruled out in this regime either with high freezing levels \nand precipitable water values around 1.8 inches. The most likely \nlocation for storms looks to be in east-central Kansas after midnight\nto around dawn though CAMs are showing some variability. Monday \nshould see clearing conditions and somewhat warmer temperatures.\n\nThe northwest flow regime stays in place through the bulk of this \nforecast period, bringing occasional chances for showers and \nthunderstorms as weak waves traverse the flow. The next best chance \nlooks to be late Tuesday into Tuesday night then again around Friday \nbut confidence is fairly low in when and where any precip will be. \nCan't rule out any severe weather or locally heavy rain but again \nnothing stands out for any particular threat at any one time. The \npattern flattens a bit this weekend and may serve to bring stronger \nsouth to southwest low-level flow for warmer and more uncomfortable \nconditions. EPS guidance continues to show the stronger positive \n850 mb temperature anomalies during this time.",
